# Rotates the local file(s) squid-ssh-stream@.service appends each branch's
# tailed access.log to (LOCAL_LOG_PATH in /etc/squid-ssh-stream/<branch>.env)
# -- without this they grow unbounded, same problem
# deploy/rsyslog/squid-branches.logrotate solves for the rsyslog+TLS method.
#
# copytruncate is required here for the same reason as that file: the
# ExecStart shell (`sh -c '... >> "$LOCAL_LOG_PATH"'`) keeps the destination
# file open for as long as the unit runs, so a rename-based rotation
# ("create" mode) would leave it writing to a now-unlinked file until the
# unit is restarted. copytruncate avoids that -- and LogTailer already
# handles the resulting truncation safely (see ARCHITECTURE.md's "Log
# tailer: rotation-safe, never crashes the process").
#
# Install on THIS (central) server -- adjust the path glob below first if
# your LOCAL_LOG_PATH values don't already live under one shared directory
# (e.g. if each branch's env file points somewhere different, list each
# path on its own line inside the braces instead of one glob). Also set
# `su` to whichever account owns that directory (the one squid-ssh-stream@
# 's User=/Group= runs as, see that unit's step 3a) -- logrotate runs as
# root by default, and refuses to touch a file under a directory that
# account itself can write to (root operating inside a non-root-owned,
# writable directory is exactly the setup a symlink-swap attack needs);
# `su` tells it to rotate as that account instead of root:
#   sudo cp deploy/systemd/squid-ssh-stream.logrotate /etc/logrotate.d/squid-ssh-stream
/home/CHANGE-ME/squid-watch/ssh-logs/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 14
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    delaycompress
    copytruncate
    su CHANGE-ME CHANGE-ME
}
